By now, it stands out clearly that the discrete conjugate-gradient algorithm is entirely similar to the scheme (I.4.70). Let us describe it for the sake of completeness:

\(1^{\circ}\)) Starting from an initial guess \(\lambda_{h}^{0}\in M_{h}\), compute the solution \(u_{h}^{0}\in X_{h}\) of the problem:

\[a_{r}^{h}(u_{h}^{0},v_{h})=\langle l,v_{h}\rangle+b(v_{h},rC_{h}^{-1}\chi- \lambda_{h}^{0})\ \ \ \ \ \forall v_{h}\in X_{h};\]

\(2^{\circ}\)) For \(m\geq 0\), knowing \((u_{h}^{m},\dot{\lambda}_{h}^{m})\in X_{h}\times M_{h}\) compute \(g_{h}^{m}\), \(\omega_{h}^{m}\in M_{h}\), \(z_{h}^{m}\in X_{h}\), \(\rho_{h}^{m}\), \(\sigma_{h}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}\) and the pair \((u_{h}^{m+1},\dot{\lambda}_{h}^{m+1})\in X_{h}\times M_{h}\) by:

\[\left\{\begin{aligned} c&(g_{h}^{m},\mu_{h})=\langle \chi_{h},\mu_{h}\rangle-b(u_{h}^{m},u_{h})&&\ \ \ \ \forall\mu_{h}\in M_{h},\\ &\sigma_{h}^{m}=\frac{c(g_{h}^{m},g_{h}^{m})}{c(g_{h}^{m-1},g_{h }^{m-1})}\\ &\omega_{h}^{m}=g_{h}^{m}+\sigma_{h}^{m}\omega_{h}^{m}\\ & a_{r}^{h}(z_{h}^{m},v_{h})=b(v_{h},\omega_{h}^{m}),\\ &\rho_{h}^{m}=\frac{c(g_{h}^{m},g_{h}^{m})}{b(z_{h}^{m},g_{h}^{ m})},\\ &\dot{\lambda}_{h}^{m+1}=\dot{\lambda}_{h}^{m}-\rho_{h}^{m} \omega_{h}^{m},\\ & u_{h}^{m+1}=u_{h}^{m}+\rho_{h}^{m}z_{h}^{m}.\end{aligned}\right.\] (1.34)

**Theorem 1.7**.: _The conjugate-gradient algorithm converges with the hypotheses of Theorem 1.5._

